up with 23.Rdxd4. After 22…Qe7 23.Rdxd4 Rc8 24.Qa4+ Rc6 25.Bc4, White comes out a pawn up (+2.76)."},{"ply":44,"name":"22…Qd7","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"22…Qd7 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 38% to 6%.","points":["White answers 23.Rxe5+, which takes the pawn on e5.","Better was 22…Qd6 (+1.36): shores up the e5 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1)."],"better":"22…Qd6 stops Rxe5+. Engine line: 23.Rf4 Bf6 24.Bg2 Rb8 25.h4 Rf8 (+1.36)."},{"ply":45,"name":"23.Rxe5+","cls":"brilliant","color":"w","headline":"23.Rxe5+!! A real sacrifice: White gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the rook on e5 where it can be taken.","Black can take, but after 23…Bxe5 24.Rxe5+ Kf8 25.Qa3+ the engine rates it +6.65.","Takes the pawn on e5.","Leaves Black with an isolated pawn on d4: a long-term weakness.","23.Rxe5+ leaves Black with an isolated pawn on d4 and takes the e5 pawn.","Takes the e5 pawn."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"23.Rxe5+!! offers the rook on e5. Taking it gives White the material back and more."},{"title":"What White gives up","text":"23.Rxe5+ puts the rook on e5 where it can be taken by the bishop on g7. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"If Black takes the rook","text":"23…Bxe5 is met by 24.Rxe5+, taking the bishop on e5 with check. Then 25.Qb4+ gives check, and 26.Qc4+ gives check. White ends up a piece and a pawn ahead: White is winning (+6.64). This is Black's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 23.Bc4, is clearly weaker (+2.28 against +6.65). Black answers 23…Kd8, preparing …Re8 and …Kc8. White would still be clearly better, but far less than after 23.Rxe5+."}]},{"ply":54,"name":"27…Qd8","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"27…Qd8 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 8% to 0%. It allows mate in 5.","points":["White answers 28.Rf7+: the rook forks the king and the pawn: both can't be saved at once.","Better was 27…Qxe7 (+6.57), which takes the rook on e7."],"better":"27…Qxe7 threatens …Qxb4, winning the queen, and trades off the attacked queen instead of losing it to Rxd7. After 28.g5+ Kxg5 29.Qxe7+ Kh6 30.Qh4+ Kg7 31.Qxd4+ Kh6, Black is a piece and a pawn down (+6.57)."}],"opening":{"name":"Sicilian Defense: Nyezhmetdinov-Rossolimo Attack","eco":"B30","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 9.g4: 17 moves in all, 9 by White and 8 by Black.","Theory runs out after 9.g4: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}